            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Pyotr Tchaikovsky</strong><br>\nPyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky was born on May 7, 1840, in Votkinsk, Russia. He was the second of six children. His father was a mining engineer, and his mother was a French-educated Russian woman who passed away when Tchaikovsky was just 14. This tragedy deeply affected him and influenced his emotional expression in music. Tchaikovsky began piano lessons at the age of five and was soon recognized for his extraordinary talent.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Tchaikovsky's Education</strong><br>\nTchaikovsky attended the Saint Petersburg Conservatory after initially training for a civil service career. He was one of the first students to enroll in the new music school. His education there exposed him to the works of Western composers, which would influence his music style. He graduated in 1865 and began to work on establishing his career as a composer.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise to Fame</strong><br>\nTchaikovsky’s first significant work, the overture \"The Storm,\" was composed in 1864. After the conservatory, his fame began to grow with the success of his compositions \"Romeo and Juliet\" and the \"Symphony No. 1 in G minor.\" These works displayed his ability to blend Russian traditional melodies with Western symphonic structure, appealing to a broad audience.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Major Works and Ballets</strong><br>\nTchaikovsky is perhaps best known for his ballets, especially \"Swan Lake,\" \"The Sleeping Beauty,\" and \"The Nutcracker.\" These compositions have become world-famous for their melodic beauty and are central to the ballet repertoire. They combine a rich use of orchestration, innovative ballet structures, and captivating music that enhances the fairy-tale narratives they are set to.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Challenges</strong><br>\nDespite his professional success, Tchaikovsky faced many personal challenges. He struggled with his mental health and his sexual orientation, which was considered taboo in Russia at the time. His marriage in 1877 to a former student, Antonina Miliukova, was a disaster and ended in a prolonged separation. Tchaikovsky’s personal turmoil often found an outlet in his deeply emotional and expressive music.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Patronage and Financial Security</strong><br>\nTchaikovsky’s financial struggles eased when he began a patronage relationship with Nadezhda von Meck, a wealthy widow who admired his work. They maintained a lengthy correspondence, though they agreed never to meet in person. Her support allowed him to quit his job and focus on composing, which led to a period of intense productivity and some of his greatest works.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
