          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Tchaikovsky as a Conductor</strong><br>\nIn addition to his composing, Tchaikovsky became a respected conductor, which helped increase the popularity of his music. He was the first Russian composer whose music made a lasting impression internationally. Tchaikovsky's conducting tours included a successful trip to the United States, where he led the New York Music Society's orchestra at the opening of Carnegie Hall in 1891.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The 1812 Overture and Other Orchestral Works</strong><br>\nThe \"1812 Overture,\" written to commemorate Russia's defense against Napoleon, is another of Tchaikovsky’s famous compositions, known for its grand finale with real cannon fire. His \"Symphony No. 4,\" \"Symphony No. 5,\" and \"Symphony No. 6 (Pathétique)\" are also highly acclaimed, with the \"Pathétique\" being particularly esteemed for its poignant emotional depths.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nTchaikovsky died on November 6, 1893, just days after the premiere of his \"Symphony No. 6,\" under mysterious circumstances that have led to much speculation. Regardless of the cause, his passing was mourned by the music community worldwide. Today, Tchaikovsky's works are celebrated for their melodic richness and are integral to classical music, with his life story continuing to fascinate music lovers and historians alike.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
